[GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") class represents a captured computation graph. [GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") objects form boundaries for expression code user writes with G-API, allowing to compile and execute it. 
G-API computations are defined with input/output data objects. G-API will track automatically which operations connect specified outputs to the inputs, forming up a call graph to be executed. The below example expresses calculation of Sobel operator for edge detection ( G=Gx2+Gy2):
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) gx = [cv::gapi::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gae1443445c4cb3187dcf439a57cfa534f)(in, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 1, 0);
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) gy = [cv::gapi::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gae1443445c4cb3187dcf439a57cfa534f)(in, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 0, 1);
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) g = [cv::gapi::sqrt](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#gaf3880c8feae9027fa91a701a1fd0d3b4)([cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(gx, gx) + [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(gy, gy));
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) out = [cv::gapi::convertTo](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d91/group__gapi__transform.html#gae972b2c91904d36e92ee14a6778db82e)(g, [CV_8U](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga32b18d904ee2b1731a9416a8eef67d06));
Full pipeline can be now captured with this object declaration:
[cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html) sobelEdge([cv::GIn](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#a8e40d34081b18c79ba4c3cfb9fd0634f)(in), [cv::GOut](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#adaa9a308669926cecc4793c6e4449629)(out));
Input/output data objects on which a call graph should be reconstructed are passed using special wrappers [cv::GIn](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#a8e40d34081b18c79ba4c3cfb9fd0634f) and [cv::GOut](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#adaa9a308669926cecc4793c6e4449629). G-API will track automatically which operations form a path from inputs to outputs and build the execution graph appropriately.
Note that [cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") doesn't take ownership on data objects it is defined. Moreover, multiple [GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") objects may be defined on the same expressions, e.g. a smaller pipeline which expects that image gradients are already pre-calculated may be defined like this:
[cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html) sobelEdgeSub([cv::GIn](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#a8e40d34081b18c79ba4c3cfb9fd0634f)(gx, gy), [cv::GOut](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#adaa9a308669926cecc4793c6e4449629)(out));
The resulting graph would expect two inputs and produce one output. In this case, it doesn't matter if gx/gy data objects are results of [cv::gapi::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gae1443445c4cb3187dcf439a57cfa534f "Calculates the first, second, third, or mixed image derivatives using an extended Sobel operator.") operators – G-API will stop unrolling expressions and building the underlying graph one reaching this data objects.
The way how [GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") is defined is important as its definition specifies graph _protocol_ – the way how the graph should be used. Protocol is defined by number of inputs, number of outputs, and shapes of inputs and outputs.
In the above example, sobelEdge expects one [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class") on input and produces one [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class"); while sobelEdgeSub expects two Mats on input and produces one [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class"). [GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...")'s protocol defines how other computation methods should be used – [cv::GComputation::compile()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#ab11bcc0578f9aa5ec8f8eaf6cda0dfa8 "Compile the computation for specific input format\(s\).") and [cv::GComputation::apply()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#aa25bc73f7cba9bb2f9992edaa248f868 "Compile graph on-the-fly and immediately execute it on the inputs data vectors."). For example, if a graph is defined on two [GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.") inputs, two [cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class") objects have to be passed to [apply()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#aa25bc73f7cba9bb2f9992edaa248f868 "Compile graph on-the-fly and immediately execute it on the inputs data vectors.") for execution. [GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") checks protocol correctness in runtime so passing a different number of objects in [apply()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#aa25bc73f7cba9bb2f9992edaa248f868 "Compile graph on-the-fly and immediately execute it on the inputs data vectors.") or passing [cv::Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d84/group__core__basic.html#ga599fe92e910c027be274233eccad7beb) instead of [cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class") there would compile well as a C++ source but raise an exception in run-time. G-API also comes with a typed wrapper cv::GComputationT<> which introduces this type-checking in compile-time.
[cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") itself is a thin object which just captures what the graph is. The compiled graph (which actually process data) is represented by class [GCompiled](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d2c/classcv_1_1GCompiled.html "Represents a compiled computation \(graph\). Can only be used with image / data formats & resolutions i..."). Use [compile()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#ab11bcc0578f9aa5ec8f8eaf6cda0dfa8 "Compile the computation for specific input format\(s\).") method to generate a compiled graph with given compile options. [cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") can also be used to process data with implicit graph compilation on-the-fly, see [apply()](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html#aa25bc73f7cba9bb2f9992edaa248f868 "Compile graph on-the-fly and immediately execute it on the inputs data vectors.") for details.
[GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") is a reference-counted object – once defined, all its copies will refer to the same instance. 

