##  Examining calcGST()
The function calcGST() is clearly an image processing pipeline:
  * It is just a sequence of operations over a number of [cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html "n-dimensional dense array class");
  * No logic (conditionals) and loops involved in the code;
  * All functions operate on 2D images (like [cv::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#gacea54f142e81b6758cb6f375ce782c8d "Calculates the first, second, third, or mixed image derivatives using an extended Sobel operator."), [cv::multiply](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga979d898a58d7f61c53003e162e7ad89f "Calculates the per-element scaled product of two arrays."), [cv::boxFilter](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#gad533230ebf2d42509547d514f7d3fbc3 "Blurs an image using the box filter."), cv::sqrt, etc).


Considering the above, calcGST() is a great candidate to start with. In the original code, its prototype is defined like this:
void calcGST(const [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html)& inputImg, [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html)& imgCoherencyOut, [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html)& imgOrientationOut, int w);
With G-API, we can define it as follows:
void calcGST(const [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& inputImg, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgCoherencyOut, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgOrientationOut, int w);
It is important to understand that the new G-API based version of calcGST() will just produce a compute graph, in contrast to its original version, which actually calculates the values. This is a principal difference – G-API based functions like this are used to construct graphs, not to process the actual data.
Let's start implementing calcGST() with calculation of matrix. This is how the original code looks like:
void calcGST(const [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html)& inputImg, [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html)& imgCoherencyOut, [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html)& imgOrientationOut, int w)
{
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) img;
inputImg.[convertTo](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#adf88c60c5b4980e05bb556080916978b)(img, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c));
// GST components calculation (start)
// J = (J11 J12; J12 J22) - GST
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) imgDiffX, imgDiffY, imgDiffXY;
[Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#gacea54f142e81b6758cb6f375ce782c8d)(img, imgDiffX, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 1, 0, 3);
[Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#gacea54f142e81b6758cb6f375ce782c8d)(img, imgDiffY, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 0, 1, 3);
[multiply](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga979d898a58d7f61c53003e162e7ad89f)(imgDiffX, imgDiffY, imgDiffXY);
Here we need to declare output objects for every new operation (see img as a result for [cv::Mat::convertTo](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#adf88c60c5b4980e05bb556080916978b "Converts an array to another data type with optional scaling."), imgDiffX and others as results for [cv::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#gacea54f142e81b6758cb6f375ce782c8d "Calculates the first, second, third, or mixed image derivatives using an extended Sobel operator.") and [cv::multiply](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga979d898a58d7f61c53003e162e7ad89f "Calculates the per-element scaled product of two arrays.")).
The G-API analogue is listed below:
void calcGST(const [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& inputImg, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgCoherencyOut, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgOrientationOut, int w)
{
auto img = [cv::gapi::convertTo](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d91/group__gapi__transform.html#gae972b2c91904d36e92ee14a6778db82e)(inputImg, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c));
auto imgDiffX = [cv::gapi::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gae1443445c4cb3187dcf439a57cfa534f)(img, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 1, 0, 3);
auto imgDiffY = [cv::gapi::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gae1443445c4cb3187dcf439a57cfa534f)(img, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 0, 1, 3);
auto imgDiffXY = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(imgDiffX, imgDiffY);
This snippet demonstrates the following syntactic difference between G-API and traditional OpenCV:
  * All standard G-API functions are by default placed in "cv::gapi" namespace;
  * G-API operations _return_ its results – there's no need to pass extra "output" parameters to the functions.


Note – this code is also using `auto` – types of intermediate objects like `img`, `imgDiffX`, and so on are inferred automatically by the C++ compiler. In this example, the types are determined by G-API operation return values which all are [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html "GMat class represents image or tensor data in the graph.").
G-API standard kernels are trying to follow OpenCV API conventions whenever possible – so cv::gapi::sobel takes the same arguments as [cv::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#gacea54f142e81b6758cb6f375ce782c8d "Calculates the first, second, third, or mixed image derivatives using an extended Sobel operator."), [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51 "Calculates the per-element scaled product of two matrices.") follows [cv::multiply](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga979d898a58d7f61c53003e162e7ad89f "Calculates the per-element scaled product of two arrays."), and so on (except having a return value).
The rest of calcGST() function can be implemented the same way trivially. Below is its full source code:
void calcGST(const [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& inputImg, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgCoherencyOut, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgOrientationOut, int w)
{
auto img = [cv::gapi::convertTo](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d91/group__gapi__transform.html#gae972b2c91904d36e92ee14a6778db82e)(inputImg, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c));
auto imgDiffX = [cv::gapi::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gae1443445c4cb3187dcf439a57cfa534f)(img, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 1, 0, 3);
auto imgDiffY = [cv::gapi::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gae1443445c4cb3187dcf439a57cfa534f)(img, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 0, 1, 3);
auto imgDiffXY = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(imgDiffX, imgDiffY);
auto imgDiffXX = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(imgDiffX, imgDiffX);
auto imgDiffYY = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(imgDiffY, imgDiffY);
auto J11 = [cv::gapi::boxFilter](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gaa37a58b30753913cbcc7f5425726dbd5)(imgDiffXX, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), [cv::Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d50/classcv_1_1Size__.html)(w, w));
auto J22 = [cv::gapi::boxFilter](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gaa37a58b30753913cbcc7f5425726dbd5)(imgDiffYY, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), [cv::Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d50/classcv_1_1Size__.html)(w, w));
auto J12 = [cv::gapi::boxFilter](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gaa37a58b30753913cbcc7f5425726dbd5)(imgDiffXY, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), [cv::Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d50/classcv_1_1Size__.html)(w, w));
auto tmp1 = J11 + J22;
auto tmp2 = J11 - J22;
auto tmp22 = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(tmp2, tmp2);
auto tmp3 = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(J12, J12);
auto tmp4 = [cv::gapi::sqrt](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#gaf3880c8feae9027fa91a701a1fd0d3b4)(tmp22 + 4.0*tmp3);
auto lambda1 = tmp1 + tmp4;
auto lambda2 = tmp1 - tmp4;
imgCoherencyOut = (lambda1 - lambda2) / (lambda1 + lambda2);
imgOrientationOut = 0.5*[cv::gapi::phase](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga3c98a8ad1054d296500220737c04d18c)(J22 - J11, 2.0*J12, true);
}
##  Running G-API graph
After calcGST() is defined in G-API language, we can construct a graph based on it and finally run it – pass input image and obtain result. Before we do it, let's have a look how original code looked like:
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) imgCoherency, imgOrientation;
calcGST(imgIn, imgCoherency, imgOrientation, W);
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) imgCoherencyBin;
imgCoherencyBin = imgCoherency > C_Thr;
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) imgOrientationBin;
[inRange](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga48af0ab51e36436c5d04340e036ce981)(imgOrientation, [Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html)(LowThr), [Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html)(HighThr), imgOrientationBin);
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) imgBin;
imgBin = imgCoherencyBin & imgOrientationBin;
[normalize](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga87eef7ee3970f86906d69a92cbf064bd)(imgCoherency, imgCoherency, 0, 255, NORM_MINMAX, [CV_8U](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga32b18d904ee2b1731a9416a8eef67d06));
[normalize](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ga87eef7ee3970f86906d69a92cbf064bd)(imgOrientation, imgOrientation, 0, 255, NORM_MINMAX, [CV_8U](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga32b18d904ee2b1731a9416a8eef67d06));
[imshow](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d7/dfc/group__highgui.html#ga453d42fe4cb60e5723281a89973ee563)("Original", imgIn);
[imshow](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d7/dfc/group__highgui.html#ga453d42fe4cb60e5723281a89973ee563)("Result", 0.5 * (imgIn + imgBin));
[imshow](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d7/dfc/group__highgui.html#ga453d42fe4cb60e5723281a89973ee563)("Coherency", imgCoherency);
[imshow](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d7/dfc/group__highgui.html#ga453d42fe4cb60e5723281a89973ee563)("Orientation", imgOrientation);
[imwrite](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#ga8ac397bd09e48851665edbe12aa28f25)("result.jpg", 0.5*(imgIn + imgBin));
[imwrite](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#ga8ac397bd09e48851665edbe12aa28f25)("Coherency.jpg", imgCoherency);
[imwrite](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#ga8ac397bd09e48851665edbe12aa28f25)("Orientation.jpg", imgOrientation);
G-API-based functions like calcGST() can't be applied to input data directly, since it is a _construction_ code, not the _processing_ code. In order to _run_ computations, a special object of class [cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") needs to be created. This object wraps our G-API code (which is a composition of G-API data and operations) into a callable object, similar to C++11 
[cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") class has a number of constructors which can be used to define a graph. Generally, user needs to pass graph boundaries – _input_ and _output_ objects, on which a GComputation is defined. Then G-API analyzes the call flow from _outputs_ to _inputs_ and reconstructs the graph with operations in-between the specified boundaries. This may sound complex, however in fact the code looks like this:
// Calculate Gradient Structure Tensor and post-process it for output with G-API
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgCoherency, imgOrientation;
calcGST(in, imgCoherency, imgOrientation, W);
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgCoherencyBin = imgCoherency > C_Thr;
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgOrientationBin = [cv::gapi::inRange](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d86/group__gapi__matrixop.html#ga76246465115220c5e5986a55298300c7)(imgOrientation, LowThr, HighThr);
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgBin = imgCoherencyBin & imgOrientationBin;
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) out = [cv::gapi::addWeighted](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d86/group__gapi__matrixop.html#ga60503b0500750881cc58a60e7eae084b)(in, 0.5, imgBin, 0.5, 0.0);
// Normalize extra outputs
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgCoherencyNorm = [cv::gapi::normalize](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d91/group__gapi__transform.html#gaedda08934a12c8b4d8f3c1762f27cacc)(imgCoherency, 0, 255, [cv::NORM_MINMAX](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ggad12cefbcb5291cf958a85b4b67b6149fa9f0c1c342a18114d47b516a88e29822e));
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgOrientationNorm = [cv::gapi::normalize](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d91/group__gapi__transform.html#gaedda08934a12c8b4d8f3c1762f27cacc)(imgOrientation, 0, 255, [cv::NORM_MINMAX](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ggad12cefbcb5291cf958a85b4b67b6149fa9f0c1c342a18114d47b516a88e29822e));
// Capture the graph into object segm
[cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html) segm([cv::GIn](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#a8e40d34081b18c79ba4c3cfb9fd0634f)(in), [cv::GOut](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#adaa9a308669926cecc4793c6e4449629)(out, imgCoherencyNorm, imgOrientationNorm));
// Define cv::Mats for output data
[cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) imgOut, imgOutCoherency, imgOutOrientation;
// Run the graph
segm.apply([cv::gin](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#a4941eb8cbe70b8c7187bc8459af7653b)(imgIn), [cv::gout](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#ac82b8a261b82157293b603b55c096a9e)(imgOut, imgOutCoherency, imgOutOrientation));
[cv::imwrite](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#ga8ac397bd09e48851665edbe12aa28f25)("result.jpg", imgOut);
[cv::imwrite](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#ga8ac397bd09e48851665edbe12aa28f25)("Coherency.jpg", imgOutCoherency);
[cv::imwrite](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#ga8ac397bd09e48851665edbe12aa28f25)("Orientation.jpg", imgOutOrientation);
Note that this code slightly changes from the original one: forming up the resulting image is also a part of the pipeline (done with [cv::gapi::addWeighted](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d86/group__gapi__matrixop.html#ga60503b0500750881cc58a60e7eae084b "Calculates the weighted sum of two matrices.")).
