##  G-API initial version: full listing
Below is the full listing of the initial anisotropic image segmentation port on G-API:
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>
#include "[opencv2/imgproc.hpp](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d4f/imgproc_2include_2opencv2_2imgproc_8hpp.html)"
#include "[opencv2/imgcodecs.hpp](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d87/imgcodecs_8hpp.html)"
#include "[opencv2/gapi.hpp](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d7/d20/gapi_8hpp.html)"
#include "[opencv2/gapi/core.hpp](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/df9/gapi_2include_2opencv2_2gapi_2core_8hpp.html)"
#include "[opencv2/gapi/imgproc.hpp](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d75/gapi_2include_2opencv2_2gapi_2imgproc_8hpp.html)"
void calcGST(const [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& inputImg, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgCoherencyOut, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgOrientationOut, int w);
int [main](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d89/highgui__qt_8cpp.html#a0ddf1224851353fc92bfbff6f499fa97)()
{
int W = 52; // window size is WxW
double C_Thr = 0.43; // threshold for coherency
int LowThr = 35; // threshold1 for orientation, it ranges from 0 to 180
int HighThr = 57; // threshold2 for orientation, it ranges from 0 to 180
[cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) imgIn = [cv::imread](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#gacbaa02cffc4ec2422dfa2e24412a99e2)("input.jpg", [cv::IMREAD_GRAYSCALE](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d8/d6a/group__imgcodecs__flags.html#gga61d9b0126a3e57d9277ac48327799c80ae29981cfc153d3b0cef5c0daeedd2125));
if (imgIn.[empty](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#abbec3525a852e77998aba034813fded4)()) //check whether the image is loaded or not
{
std::cout << "ERROR : Image cannot be loaded..!!" << std::endl;
return -1;
}
// Calculate Gradient Structure Tensor and post-process it for output with G-API
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgCoherency, imgOrientation;
calcGST(in, imgCoherency, imgOrientation, W);
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgCoherencyBin = imgCoherency > C_Thr;
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgOrientationBin = [cv::gapi::inRange](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d86/group__gapi__matrixop.html#ga76246465115220c5e5986a55298300c7)(imgOrientation, LowThr, HighThr);
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgBin = imgCoherencyBin & imgOrientationBin;
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) out = [cv::gapi::addWeighted](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d86/group__gapi__matrixop.html#ga60503b0500750881cc58a60e7eae084b)(in, 0.5, imgBin, 0.5, 0.0);
// Normalize extra outputs
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgCoherencyNorm = [cv::gapi::normalize](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d91/group__gapi__transform.html#gaedda08934a12c8b4d8f3c1762f27cacc)(imgCoherency, 0, 255, [cv::NORM_MINMAX](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ggad12cefbcb5291cf958a85b4b67b6149fa9f0c1c342a18114d47b516a88e29822e));
[cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html) imgOrientationNorm = [cv::gapi::normalize](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d91/group__gapi__transform.html#gaedda08934a12c8b4d8f3c1762f27cacc)(imgOrientation, 0, 255, [cv::NORM_MINMAX](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html#ggad12cefbcb5291cf958a85b4b67b6149fa9f0c1c342a18114d47b516a88e29822e));
// Capture the graph into object segm
[cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html) segm([cv::GIn](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#a8e40d34081b18c79ba4c3cfb9fd0634f)(in), [cv::GOut](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#adaa9a308669926cecc4793c6e4449629)(out, imgCoherencyNorm, imgOrientationNorm));
// Define cv::Mats for output data
[cv::Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) imgOut, imgOutCoherency, imgOutOrientation;
// Run the graph
segm.apply([cv::gin](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#a4941eb8cbe70b8c7187bc8459af7653b)(imgIn), [cv::gout](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/d75/namespacecv.html#ac82b8a261b82157293b603b55c096a9e)(imgOut, imgOutCoherency, imgOutOrientation));
[cv::imwrite](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#ga8ac397bd09e48851665edbe12aa28f25)("result.jpg", imgOut);
[cv::imwrite](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#ga8ac397bd09e48851665edbe12aa28f25)("Coherency.jpg", imgOutCoherency);
[cv::imwrite](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#ga8ac397bd09e48851665edbe12aa28f25)("Orientation.jpg", imgOutOrientation);
return 0;
}
void calcGST(const [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& inputImg, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgCoherencyOut, [cv::GMat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/df/daa/classcv_1_1GMat.html)& imgOrientationOut, int w)
{
auto img = [cv::gapi::convertTo](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d91/group__gapi__transform.html#gae972b2c91904d36e92ee14a6778db82e)(inputImg, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c));
auto imgDiffX = [cv::gapi::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gae1443445c4cb3187dcf439a57cfa534f)(img, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 1, 0, 3);
auto imgDiffY = [cv::gapi::Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gae1443445c4cb3187dcf439a57cfa534f)(img, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 0, 1, 3);
auto imgDiffXY = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(imgDiffX, imgDiffY);
auto imgDiffXX = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(imgDiffX, imgDiffX);
auto imgDiffYY = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(imgDiffY, imgDiffY);
auto J11 = [cv::gapi::boxFilter](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gaa37a58b30753913cbcc7f5425726dbd5)(imgDiffXX, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), [cv::Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d50/classcv_1_1Size__.html)(w, w));
auto J22 = [cv::gapi::boxFilter](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gaa37a58b30753913cbcc7f5425726dbd5)(imgDiffYY, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), [cv::Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d50/classcv_1_1Size__.html)(w, w));
auto J12 = [cv::gapi::boxFilter](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dc5/group__gapi__filters.html#gaa37a58b30753913cbcc7f5425726dbd5)(imgDiffXY, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), [cv::Size](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d50/classcv_1_1Size__.html)(w, w));
auto tmp1 = J11 + J22;
auto tmp2 = J11 - J22;
auto tmp22 = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(tmp2, tmp2);
auto tmp3 = [cv::gapi::mul](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga42a8187b2bf350f90e4a31f93e929e51)(J12, J12);
auto tmp4 = [cv::gapi::sqrt](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#gaf3880c8feae9027fa91a701a1fd0d3b4)(tmp22 + 4.0*tmp3);
auto lambda1 = tmp1 + tmp4;
auto lambda2 = tmp1 - tmp4;
imgCoherencyOut = (lambda1 - lambda2) / (lambda1 + lambda2);
imgOrientationOut = 0.5*[cv::gapi::phase](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/dd3/group__gapi__math.html#ga3c98a8ad1054d296500220737c04d18c)(J22 - J11, 2.0*J12, true);
}
#  Inspecting the initial version
After we have got the initial working version of our algorithm working with G-API, we can use it to inspect and learn how G-API works. This chapter covers two aspects: understanding the graph structure, and memory profiling.
##  Understanding the graph structure
G-API stands for "Graph API", but did you mention any graphs in the above example? It was one of the initial design goals – G-API was designed with expressions in mind to make adoption and porting process more straightforward. People _usually_ don't think in terms of _Nodes_ and _Edges_ when writing ordinary code, and so G-API, while being a Graph API, doesn't force its users to do that.
However, a graph is still built implicitly when a [cv::GComputation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/dfe/classcv_1_1GComputation.html "GComputation class represents a captured computation graph. GComputation objects form boundaries for ...") object is defined. It may be useful to inspect how the resulting graph looks like to check if it is generated correctly and if it really represents our algorithm. It is also useful to learn the structure of the graph to see if it has any redundancies.
G-API allows to dump generated graphs to `.dot` files which then could be visualized with 
In order to dump our graph to a `.dot` file, set `GRAPH_DUMP_PATH` to a file name before running the application, e.g.: 
Now this file can be visualized with a `dot` command like this: 
or viewed interactively with `xdot` (please refer to your distribution/operating system documentation on how to install these packages).
Anisotropic image segmentation graph
The above diagram demonstrates a number of interesting aspects of G-API's internal algorithm representation:
  1. G-API underlying graph is a bipartite graph: it consists of _Operation_ and _Data_ nodes such that a _Data_ node can only be connected to an _Operation_ node, _Operation_ node can only be connected to a _Data_ node, and nodes of a single kind are never connected directly.
  2. Graph is directed - every edge in the graph has a direction.
  3. Graph "begins" and "ends" with a _Data_ kind of nodes.
  4. A _Data_ node can have only a single writer and multiple readers.
  5. An _Operation_ node may have multiple inputs, though every input must have an unique _port number_ (among inputs).
  6. An _Operation_ node may have multiple outputs, and every output must have an unique _port number_ (among outputs).


