#  Basic operations with images
##  Accessing pixel intensity valuesC++JavaPython
In order to get pixel intensity value, you have to know the type of an image and the number of channels. Here is an example for a single channel grey scale image (type 8UC1) and pixel coordinates x and y:
C++
[Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html) intensity = img.at<[uchar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga65f85814a8290f9797005d3b28e7e5fc)>(y, x);
Java
byte[] imgData = new byte[(int) (img.total() * img.channels())];
img.get(0, 0, imgData);
byte intensity = imgData[y * img.cols() + x];
Python
_intensity = img[y,x]
C++ version only: intensity.val[0] contains a value from 0 to 255. Note the ordering of x and y. Since in OpenCV images are represented by the same structure as matrices, we use the same convention for both cases - the 0-based row index (or y-coordinate) goes first and the 0-based column index (or x-coordinate) follows it. Alternatively, you can use the following notation (**C++ only**):
[Scalar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/da0/classcv_1_1Scalar__.html) intensity = img.at<[uchar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga65f85814a8290f9797005d3b28e7e5fc)>([Point](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/d4e/classcv_1_1Point__.html)(x, y));
Now let us consider a 3 channel image with BGR color ordering (the default format returned by imread):
**C++ code**
[Vec3b](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/dcf/classcv_1_1Vec.html) intensity = img.at<[Vec3b](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/dcf/classcv_1_1Vec.html)>(y, x);
[uchar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga65f85814a8290f9797005d3b28e7e5fc) blue = intensity.[val](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/de/de1/classcv_1_1Matx.html#afa9ad3bc5b09ebcdfc6f98f44c15191d)[0];
[uchar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga65f85814a8290f9797005d3b28e7e5fc) green = intensity.[val](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/de/de1/classcv_1_1Matx.html#afa9ad3bc5b09ebcdfc6f98f44c15191d)[1];
[uchar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga65f85814a8290f9797005d3b28e7e5fc) red = intensity.[val](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/de/de1/classcv_1_1Matx.html#afa9ad3bc5b09ebcdfc6f98f44c15191d)[2];
**Python Python**
_blue = img[y,x,0]
_green = img[y,x,1]
_red = img[y,x,2]
You can use the same method for floating-point images (for example, you can get such an image by running Sobel on a 3 channel image) (**C++ only**):
[Vec3f](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/dcf/classcv_1_1Vec.html) intensity = img.at<[Vec3f](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/dcf/classcv_1_1Vec.html)>(y, x);
float blue = intensity.[val](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/de/de1/classcv_1_1Matx.html#afa9ad3bc5b09ebcdfc6f98f44c15191d)[0];
float green = intensity.[val](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/de/de1/classcv_1_1Matx.html#afa9ad3bc5b09ebcdfc6f98f44c15191d)[1];
float red = intensity.[val](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/de/de1/classcv_1_1Matx.html#afa9ad3bc5b09ebcdfc6f98f44c15191d)[2];
The same method can be used to change pixel intensities:
C++
img.at<[uchar](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga65f85814a8290f9797005d3b28e7e5fc)>(y, x) = 128;
Java
byte[] imgData = new byte[(int) (img.total() * img.channels())];
imgData[y * img.cols() + x] = (byte) 128;
img.put(0, 0, imgData);
Python
img[y,x] = 128
There are functions in OpenCV, especially from calib3d module, such as [cv::projectPoints](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/d0c/group__calib3d.html#ga1019495a2c8d1743ed5cc23fa0daff8c "Projects 3D points to an image plane."), that take an array of 2D or 3D points in the form of Mat. Matrix should contain exactly one column, each row corresponds to a point, matrix type should be 32FC2 or 32FC3 correspondingly. Such a matrix can be easily constructed from `std::vector` (**C++ only**):
vector<Point2f> points;
//... fill the array
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) pointsMat = [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html)(points);
One can access a point in this matrix using the same method `Mat::at` (**C++ only**):
[Point2f](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/d4e/classcv_1_1Point__.html) point = pointsMat.[at](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#aa10d804e90b51d4157b5c26e12c5d2df)<[Point2f](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/db/d4e/classcv_1_1Point__.html)>(i, 0);
##  Memory management and reference countingC++JavaPython
Mat is a structure that keeps matrix/image characteristics (rows and columns number, data type etc) and a pointer to data. So nothing prevents us from having several instances of Mat corresponding to the same data. A Mat keeps a reference count that tells if data has to be deallocated when a particular instance of Mat is destroyed. Here is an example of creating two matrices without copying data (**C++ only**):
std::vector<Point3f> points;
// .. fill the array
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) pointsMat = [Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html)(points).[reshape](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a4eb96e3251417fa88b78e2abd6cfd7d8)(1);
As a result, we get a 32FC1 matrix with 3 columns instead of 32FC3 matrix with 1 column. `pointsMat` uses data from points and will not deallocate the memory when destroyed. In this particular instance, however, developer has to make sure that lifetime of `points` is longer than of `pointsMat` If we need to copy the data, this is done using, for example, [cv::Mat::copyTo](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a33fd5d125b4c302b0c9aa86980791a77 "Copies the matrix to another one.") or [cv::Mat::clone](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a03d2a2570d06dcae378f788725789aa4 "Creates a full copy of the array and the underlying data."):
C++
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) img = [imread](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#gacbaa02cffc4ec2422dfa2e24412a99e2)("image.jpg");
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) img1 = img.[clone](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html#a03d2a2570d06dcae378f788725789aa4)();
Java
Mat img = Imgcodecs.imread("image.jpg");
Mat img1 = img.clone();
Python
img = [cv.imread](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#gacbaa02cffc4ec2422dfa2e24412a99e2)('image.jpg')
_img1 = np.copy(img)
An empty output Mat can be supplied to each function. Each implementation calls Mat::create for a destination matrix. This method allocates data for a matrix if it is empty. If it is not empty and has the correct size and type, the method does nothing. If however, size or type are different from the input arguments, the data is deallocated (and lost) and a new data is allocated. For example:
C++
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) img = [imread](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#gacbaa02cffc4ec2422dfa2e24412a99e2)("image.jpg");
[Mat](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d3/d63/classcv_1_1Mat.html) sobelx;
[Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#gacea54f142e81b6758cb6f375ce782c8d)(img, sobelx, [CV_32F](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/d1b/group__core__hal__interface.html#ga4a3def5d72b74bed31f5f8ab7676099c), 1, 0);
Java
Mat img = Imgcodecs.imread("image.jpg");
Mat sobelx = new Mat();
Imgproc.Sobel(img, sobelx, CvType.CV_32F, 1, 0);
Python
img = [cv.imread](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/da8/group__imgcodecs.html#gacbaa02cffc4ec2422dfa2e24412a99e2)('image.jpg')
_sobelx = [cv.Sobel](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html#gacea54f142e81b6758cb6f375ce782c8d)(img, cv.CV_32F, 1, 0)
